==English==

===Etymology 1===
{{wikidata lexeme|L5719}}
From {{inh|en|enm|accesse}}, {{m|enm|acces}}, borrowed from {{der|en|frm|acces||attack, onslaught}} or from its source {{der|en|la|accessus}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|accēdō||approach; accede}}, from {{m|la|ad||to, toward, at}} + {{m|la|cēdō||move, yield}}. {{doublet|en|accessus}}. First attested in the early 14th century. The sense "entrance" was first attested about 1380.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæksɛs/|a=RP}}
* {{enPR|ăkʹsĕs'|a=GA}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈækˌsɛs/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-access.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|æksɛs|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|ac|cess}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A [[way]] or [[means]] of [[approach]]ing or [[enter]]ing; an [[entrance]]; a [[passage]].
#: {{ux|en|The door provides '''access''' to the premises.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|passage=All '''access''' was thronged.|year=1873}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The act of approaching or entering; an [[advance]].
#: {{ux|en|They gained '''access''' to the basement from the stairs.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The right or ability of approaching or entering; [[admittance]]; [[admission]]; [[accessibility]].
#: {{ux|en|Staff prevent unauthorized '''access''' to the building.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The quality of being easy to approach or enter.
#: {{co|en|ease of '''access'''}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|2|1|passage=I did repel his fetters, and denied His '''access''' to me.}}
#* {{RQ:Telegraph|date=September 20, 2011| author=Graeme Paton| title=University access plan 'will fail', says Russell Group| url=http://www.telegraph.co.uk/education/universityeducation/8774824/University-access-plan-will-fail-says-Russell-Group.html| passage=Coalition plans to widen '''access''' to university will fail to get to the 'root cause' of the problem, according to the Russell Group.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20241107003618/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/education/universityeducation/8774824/University-access-plan-will-fail-says-Russell-Group.html|archivedate=7 November 2024}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} Admission to [[sexual]] [[intercourse]].
#* {{RQ:Blackstone Commentaries|passage=During [[coverture]], '''access''' of the husband shall be presumed, unless the contrary be shown.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|countable}} An [[increase]] by [[addition]]; [[accession]].
#: {{co|en|an '''access''' of territory}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|passage=I, from the influence of thy looks, receive '''access''' in every virtue.|year=1873}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} An [[onset]], [[attack]], or [[fit]] of disease; an [[ague]] fit.
#* {{RQ:Burnet History|text=The first '''access''' looked like an apoplexy.}}
#* {{RQ:Beckett Watt|text=Then he resumed the pose, the decent pose, from which the sudden '''access''' of his old trouble had startled him, his hands on his knees, {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} An [[outburst]] of an [[emotion]]; a [[paroxysm]]; a fit of passion.
#: {{syn|en|accession|q1=literary}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1917|author=w:Frank L. Packard|title=The Adventures of Jimmie Dale| chapter=15| passage=The Magpie's flashlight, as he shifted it from his right hand to his left and wrenched out his revolver, had fallen upon two men crouched close against the wall by the library door—and he screamed out in an '''access''' of fury. "De double cross! A plant! De bulls! You damned snitch, Larry!" screamed out the Magpie—and fired.}}
#* '''1946''', {{w|Arnold J. Toynbee}}, ''A Study of History'' (Abridgement of Volumes I-VI by D.C. Somervell)
#*: It appears that, about the middle of the fourth century of the Christian Era, the Germans in the Roman service started the new practice of retaining their native names; and this change of etiquette, which seems to have been abrupt, points to a sudden '''access''' of self-confidence and self-assurance in the souls of the barbarian ''personnel'' which had previously been content to 'go Roman' without reservations.
#* {{see more citations|en}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|legal}} The right of a [[noncustodial parent]] to visit their [[child]].
# {{lb|en|countable|computing}} The process of locating data in memory.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Victor Pankratius; Ali-Reza Adl-Tabatabai; Walter Tichy|title=Fundamentals of Multicore Software Development|page=74
|passage=Operations on C++ volatiles do put the compiler on notice that the object may be modified asynchronously, and hence are generally safer to use than ordinary variable '''accesses'''.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|networking}} [[connection|Connection]] to or [[communication]] with a [[computer program]] or to the [[Internet]].
# {{lb|en|uncountable|Scotland}} {{cap|complicity}} or [[assent]].

